          "title": "You'll want to know: iuventūs, iuventūtis",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "iuventūs, iuventūtis — \"youth; young people\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Tōta iuventūs urbis in campō lūdēbat. All the young people of the city were playing on the field."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine, 3rd declension, from iuvenis, 'young'. Both the time of youth and the young people themselves."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "iuventūs is built on iuvenis, \"young\" (EtymDB), which is inherited and a cousin of English young. English juvenile comes from iuvenis."
            }
          ]
        },
